ADDITIONS={
'D12':('Static analysis and its limits',[("Characteristic checks","Tools detect patterns such as uninitialized variables, unreachable code, inconsistent parameters and memory leaks."),("Custom rules and assertions","User-defined rules extend checks. Assertion checking uses specified relationships to find possible violations."),("Evidence boundary","A warning identifies a possible defect; absence of warnings does not establish system safety. Combine analysis with inspections, tests and a hazard argument.")],50,53,'Why is a clean static-analysis report insufficient for safety?','The analyzer checks a model and selected defect classes. Requirements, physical hazards, omitted behavior and operating assumptions may remain outside those checks.'),
'D14':('Operational processes and human recovery',[("Process design","People need accessible information, training and authority to cope with disruptions. Check personal IT as well as enterprise IT dependencies."),("Automation","Automation can speed routine work but may reduce situational awareness and manual recovery capability. Test the handover when automation fails."),("Efficiency trade-off","Spare capacity, rehearsal and diversity have a cost. Relate that cost to the critical service and the failure modes the organization must survive.")],33,41,'What should a recovery rehearsal measure besides server restart time?','Measure whether people can restore the essential service, verify data, coordinate responsibilities and operate a safe fallback under the stated disruption.'),
'D15':('Reuse planning and configuration',[("Six planning factors","Consider schedule, expected lifetime, team experience, criticality and non-functional requirements, application domain and execution platform."),("Product-line specialization","Variation can target platform, environment, functionality or business process. Define what is shared and what varies."),("Deployment configuration","Select components, define workflows and validation rules, and set parameters. Configuration still needs validation against the organization’s requirements.")],15,40,'A configurable product fits the workflow but cannot meet the required response time. Is configuration enough?','No. A functional fit does not establish non-functional suitability. Obtain workload-specific evidence, negotiate a bounded requirement or choose a different approach.'),
'D16':('Interface contracts and OCL',[("Precondition","The caller must establish the condition before an operation. For addItem, the supplied example requires an existing library and an unused photo identifier."),("Postcondition","After addItem, the library size increases by one, the identifier retrieves the added photo and the catalogue entry matches the descriptor."),("State before execution","OCL @pre refers to the earlier value. The supplied delete contract removes the photo and decreases size, but preserves its catalogue entry. That choice must be understood by both components.")],51,55,'Why can deleting a photo preserve its catalogue entry without violating the supplied contract?','The postcondition explicitly preserves catEntry(pid) at its pre-call value. A caller that expects both records to disappear has a semantic mismatch despite a compatible signature.'),
'D16B':('Composition trade-offs and contract tests',[("Functional fit","Choose a composition that delivers the needed services and respects provides/requires contracts."),("Non-functional behavior","Latency, reliability and security of the composition depend on interactions. Component-level claims need integration evidence."),("Evolution","Compare delivery speed with replaceability and future change. Record any requirement modified to fit an available component.")],56,59,'Give one positive and one negative test for addItem.','Positive: use an unused identifier and verify photo, descriptor and size increment. Negative: attempt a duplicate identifier and check the documented rejection behavior. Do not assume an undocumented exception type.')
}
